t it  and that that's what will happen if  you've got kind of a uniform density  atmosphere that you can take something  if it's floating there you move it up  it's still floating  if on the other hand you've got a  stratified atmosphere so actually the  density decreases as you go up as the  earth's atmosphere does then what will  happen is you've got something which is  neutrally buoyant at this point and  let's get rid of the balloon let's just  have a little blob of air there  if you were to displace it upwards  suddenly it's surrounded by air that's  less dense than it is  and so it'll sink again and it'll keep  sinking it'll actually sink through its  original point and then it'll find  itself in a region where it's less dense  than the stuff around it and now it'll  start to rise and so it will actually  oscillate backwards and forwards and the  frequency at which it oscillates  backwards and forwards is this thing  called the brunt visalus frequency and  the reason why that has to do with these  lines of clouds is that if you have uh  some air with some moisture in it if it  starts undergoing these oscillations  then as it rises it'll cool and suddenly  it'll cool to the point where clouds  condense and so you'll actually make  clouds and then when it sinks again  those clouds will then evaporate again  and the clouds will disappear so if  you've got air which is traveling along  and undergoing these oscillations you'll  get clouds then the clouds will  disappear then the clouds will appear  again then the clouds will disappear  again in the atmosphere the period of  this oscillation is something it's of  order 100 seconds typically if you look  at the how the the atmosphere's  stratified and use all the physical  parameters it works out about 100  seconds and typically air is traveling  at maybe 10 meters per second  normal kind of wind speed so  in each oscillation it will have  traveled about 10 meters per second over  100 seconds you travel a kilometer and  that's why the scale of these clouds is  typically a kilometer or so that you'll  get a cloud then a gap then a cloud and  there's typical spacing between them is  of order a kilometer so it's just  depositing a little cloud at the top of  its wave  it's actually well the same air will  form the cloud and then the cloud will  disappear again so you basically create  the cloud and then the cloud disappears  and the cloud appears so you're sort of  seeing those high peaks when it goes up  it forms the cloud and it's wherever  you're at at the top you'll get the  cloud and then it disappears again and  then reappears again  won't that oscillation fade away when it  eventually stop oscillating and just  sort of  stop like re like a like a pendulum  eventually runs out of puff if you just  look at the mass you actually figure out  what the mass of this oscillation is it  really is i think this thing called the  harmonic oscillator and the harmonic  oscillator will just keep oscillating  forever now obviously there are other  effects going on as well  so there are will be damping terms which  means that actually one of these waves  won't actually last forever but it  really can persist for many many  oscillations so actually you can have  this whole series of clouds being  created in that way so this frequency  comes up all over the place so it comes  up in oceans because you can have  exactly the same effects in oceans of  pockets of water rising and falling in  stars you can have little bits of the  star in the stellar atmosphere rising  and falling and they're all div driven  by the same frequency this by silent  frequency  and again something here again you can  see the net pull of the earth and the  sun is all nicely lined up which will  keep it in an orbit basically following  following the earth around there used to  be lots of science fiction stories about  l3 it used to be a popular place to put  the anti-earth or you know if you're an  evil genius that's where you put your  hidden layer

